<%# The wall-tablet surface: the pet fills the top half (that's the ordinary
    hero, just given the room), and this is everything below the fold.

    Shows the same set the Quick popover does — every routine that's switched
    on, starred ones first — so there's nothing kiosk-specific to curate. Save
    a routine and it's on the wall; star it to move it to the front. %>
<section class="byte-kiosk" data-byte-kiosk>
  <%# The pet's speech bubble (`data-kiosk-say`) now lives up in the hero, by
      its head — see byte/show.html.erb. A tap here has no thread to scroll, so
      that bubble is still the only place a receipt lands. %>

  <%# Board-only: a STANDING alert (the laundry gate is open, the Whisper is
      hungry). Unlike the say bubble above, this doesn't fade — it holds for as
      long as the condition stands, and the pet does its alarm shuffle beside it
      (no sound). Fed from Buddy::Alerts by buddy/board_alert.js. %>
  <% if @board %>
    <div class="byte-board-alert" data-board-alert aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
  <% end %>

  <%# A question mid-routine (ask_me, or a step that wants confirming). Same
      renderers the thread uses, so the dinner routine works here exactly as it
      does in chat.

      Above the buttons and IN FLOW, taking only the room it needs — never
      floated over them. It used to be an overlay, which meant a question that
      couldn't be answered (its action expired, or the tap just failed) sat
      there swallowing every press aimed at a routine underneath. The buttons
      on a wall tablet are the whole point; nothing gets to cover them. %>
  <div class="byte-kiosk-ask" data-kiosk-ask hidden>
    <button type="button" class="byte-kiosk-ask-close" data-kiosk-ask-close
            aria-label="Put this away" title="Put this away">×</button>
    <div data-kiosk-ask-body></div>
  </div>

  <%# What sits under the pet: either the routine pad or, on a board, one
      group of chores as circles. One or the other and never both — a wall
      screen is for walking up to and pressing the thing you came for, and two
      grids of big targets is the state where you have to read first. %>
  <% if @board %>
    <%= render "byte/chore_board" %>
  <% else %>
  <%# Server-rendered so a cold boot off the cached shell has its buttons
      immediately. kiosk.js rebuilds this from /buddy/routines on load and
      whenever the tablet comes back to the foreground, in the same shape —
      pinning something on your phone should show up here without a reload. %>
  <div class="byte-kiosk-pad" data-kiosk-pad>
    <% Array(@routines).each do |routine| %>
      <%# Title only - see paintPad in kiosk.js, which this has to match or the %>
      <%# first paint changes shape the moment the pad refreshes. %>
      <%# A monitored routine paints its saved name here and is relabelled by the %>
      <%# first broadcast. The name is what a cold boot shows for the beat before %>
      <%# the socket answers, so it wants to read as a sensible default. %>
      <button type="button" class="byte-kiosk-btn" data-kiosk-routine="<%= routine.id %>"
              <%= tag.attributes(data: { kiosk_monitor: routine.monitor }) if routine.monitor %>
              title="<%= routine.description.presence || routine.name %>"><%= routine.name %></button>
    <% end %>
    <% if Array(@routines).empty? %>
        <%# Matches NO_PINNED_ROUTINES in routine_order.js. They may well have %>
        <%# routines and just none starred, so "save one" would send them off %>
        <%# to do a thing they've already done. %>
        <p class="byte-kiosk-empty">Nothing pinned yet — star a routine to put it on this screen.</p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# Which companion is on this screen. The thread IS the answer — its theme
      decides the character, the name, the palette and the voice — so choosing
      one here is the whole of "set the Buddy".

      The control is the current pet's own face, so what it changes is legible
      without a label. `data-byte-pet-avatar` gets it repointed along with
      every other avatar when the thread switches. %>
  <%# This screen's own preferences — notifications, sound, timer alerts.
      Opens the shared settings dialog; a wall surface has no drawer to reach it
      from, so it gets its own control, grouped with the companion picker. %>
  <button type="button" class="byte-kiosk-settings" data-byte-open-settings
          aria-label="Settings" title="Settings">⚙️</button>

  <button type="button" class="byte-kiosk-who" data-kiosk-who
          aria-label="Choose which companion is on this screen"
          title="Choose which companion is on this screen">
    <%= image_tag chrome[:avatar], alt: chrome[:name], data: { byte_pet_avatar: true } %>
  </button>
  <div class="byte-kiosk-picker" data-kiosk-picker hidden>
    <span class="byte-kiosk-picker-label">On this screen</span>
    <div class="byte-kiosk-picker-list" data-kiosk-picker-list></div>
  </div>
</section>
